      "question": {
        ":question/id": "47a86e3f-7ec5-5240-9f56-6b079c551359",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x)=\\frac{2 x^{2}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the original quotient is defined.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=2 x^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2 x^{2})^{\\prime}\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 x^{2})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}}{(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(4 x)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 x^{2})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})}{(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{2 x \\left(x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
